Iridium alone wasn't enough to convince everyone. Volcanoes can bring up a little iridium from deep [[@inside-earth|inside Earth]], and India's [[@deccan|Deccan]] volcanoes were erupting around the same time. So scientists looked for more clues.

They found shocked quartz: sand grains with criss-cross scars that form only under a sudden, violent squeeze, like the one from a giant impact. They found tiny glass beads called spherules, made when melted rock was thrown into the sky and rained back down. Near the Gulf of Mexico, they found jumbled rock that looked as if giant waves had dumped it.

{{story:asteroid-size}}

गंगा और ब्रह्मपुत्र बांग्लादेश में मिलती हैं और धरती के सबसे बड़े डेल्टा से होकर बंगाल की खाड़ी में गिरती हैं। यह डेल्टा बांग्लादेश के ज़्यादातर हिस्से और भारत के पश्चिम बंगाल के एक हिस्से पर फैला है। इसकी [[fertile|उपजाऊ]] मिट्टी 10 करोड़ से भी कहीं ज़्यादा लोगों का पेट भरती है, जो यहाँ धान, जूट और सब्ज़ियाँ उगाते हैं और इसकी धाराओं में मछली पकड़ते हैं।

इसके दक्षिणी सिरे पर, जहाँ नदी समुद्र से मिलती है, सुंदरबन है, दुनिया का सबसे बड़ा [[@mangroves|मैंग्रोव]] जंगल। इसके पेड़ खारी कीचड़ में उगते हैं, और यहाँ मशहूर [[@sundarbans-tigers|बंगाल टाइगर]] रहते हैं, जो तैरकर एक टापू से दूसरे टापू जाते हैं। इसका भारतीय और बांग्लादेशी, दोनों हिस्से यूनेस्को की [[@unesco-sites|विश्व धरोहर]] हैं।

{{story:sundarbans-tigers}}

Owls don't rely on eyes alone. Many have a flat, round face called a facial disc. It works like a satellite dish, gathering sound towards the [[@ears-hear|ears]].

In barn owls, one ear opening sits higher than the other. A squeak from below reaches one ear a fraction earlier and louder than the other. In the 1960s, the American scientist Roger Payne showed that barn owls could catch mice in a completely dark room, using sound alone. Add feathers that let them fly almost [[@owl-silent|silently]], and you have one of the best night hunters there is.

{{story:ears-hear}}

Later matches used white phosphorus, which lit easily on almost anything. But it was deadly. Factory workers, many of them young women and girls, breathed in the fumes and developed a terrible bone disease in the jaw called "phossy jaw".

In 1888, the matchgirls of a London factory went on strike over pay and conditions, and won better treatment. In 1906, many countries signed an agreement to ban white phosphorus in matches. Safer chemistry won.

{{story:fire-discovery}}

In the early 1800s, the Scottish engineer John McAdam had a cheap new idea. Instead of big stones, he used small, sharp broken stones packed in layers, which locked together under passing wheels. The road was higher in the middle so water drained away. These became known as macadam roads.

Horses and carriages kicked up clouds of dust on them, though. In 1902, the British engineer Edgar Hooley patented a way of binding the stones with tar. He called it tarmac, short for tar macadam. Modern roads usually use bitumen, made from [[@oil-coal|crude oil]], instead of tar.

{{story:bicycles-history}}

[[loom|करघे]] पर हज़ारों लंबे धागे तने रहते हैं, जिन्हें ताना कहते हैं। बुनकर दूसरे धागे, जिन्हें बाना कहते हैं, उनके ऊपर-नीचे से निकालता है। नमूना बनाने के लिए ताने के कुछ खास धागों को ठीक समय पर ऊपर उठाना पड़ता है।

पुराने करघों पर एक मददगार करघे के ऊपर बैठकर डोरियाँ खींचता था, ताकि सही धागे उठें। 20वीं सदी की शुरुआत में वाराणसी के बुनकरों ने जैकार्ड करघा अपना लिया, जो छेद वाले कार्डों की लड़ियों से चलता है। हर छेद से एक धागा उठता है। एक पेचीदा साड़ी के लिए हज़ारों कार्ड लग सकते हैं। छेद होना या न होना, इसी तरीके ने बाद में शुरुआती कंप्यूटरों के पंच कार्डों को प्रेरणा दी। यह कहानी [[@binary-code|बाइनरी कोड]] में पढ़ो।

{{story:binary-code}}

In July 1944, while the [[@world-war-two|Second World War]] was still raging, delegates from 44 countries met at a hotel in Bretton Woods, in the mountains of New Hampshire, USA. India sent a delegation too, though it was still under British rule.

They wanted to avoid the mistakes of the 1930s, when countries had raised trade barriers and let their [[currency|currencies]] crash, deepening the Great Depression. The British economist John Maynard Keynes and the American official Harry Dexter White led the planning. Out of three weeks of talks came both the IMF and the bank that grew into the World Bank Group.

{{story:world-war-two}}

A [[@fastest-animal|cheetah]] can reach around 100 km/h in a short burst. India's [[@vande-bharat|Vande Bharat trains]] are built for speeds up to about 160 km/h on suitable tracks. Japan's [[@bullet-trains|bullet trains]] run at up to 320 km/h.

[[@space-weird-sound|Sound travels through air]] at about 343 metres per second, which is about 1,235 km/h. That's why you see [[@lightning|lightning]] before you hear [[@thunder-sound|thunder]]. Light is far faster: about 3 lakh km every second. It could go round the Earth more than seven times in one second.

{{story:bullet-trains}}

The building's caretaker could not find boxes, so he brought two old peach baskets. Naismith nailed them to the railing of the gym's running track. The railing happened to be 10 feet above the floor, about 3 metres.

That accident became the rule. The hoop is still exactly 10 feet high in schools, colleges and the NBA, more than 130 years later.

{{story:gravity-fall}}

Early [[@first-computers|computers]] filled whole rooms and cost more than a house. Nobody bought one to play games. The game ran on a PDP-1, a machine about the size of a couple of big refrigerators. Students wrote it in their spare time, as long lists of instructions that the machine stored in [[@binary-code|binary code]]. They shared the code freely, so copies spread to other universities.

The game had real physics. The star in the middle pulled ships toward it with [[gravity|gravity]], and players had to steer around it. Many people who later built the computer industry first touched a computer by playing it late at night.

{{story:first-computers}}

1862 में नीदरलैंड के आँखों के डॉक्टर हरमन स्नेलन ने एक चार्ट बनाया, जिसमें अक्षरों की कतारें नीचे की ओर छोटी होती जाती हैं। इसके अलग-अलग रूप आज भी दुनिया भर के क्लिनिकों में टँगे हैं। अमेरिका में "20/20 नज़र" कहा जाता है, जिसका मतलब है कि आम आँख 20 फ़ुट से जो पढ़ सकती है, वह तुम भी 20 फ़ुट से पढ़ लेते हो। भारत जैसे मीटर वाले देशों में इसी बात को 6/6 लिखते हैं, यानी 6 मीटर।

बच्चों को हमेशा पता नहीं चलता कि उन्हें धुँधला दिख रहा है, क्योंकि वे सोचते हैं कि सबको ऐसा ही दिखता है। स्कूल में आँखों की जाँच से ऐसी दिक्कतें जल्दी पकड़ में आ जाती हैं।

{{story:lenses-glasses}}

A farmer standing at the edge of a field sees the nearest rows. A drone flying overhead sees all of it. Cameras on drones and satellites can detect light our eyes miss, which shows whether leaves are healthy and green inside or starting to struggle. A yellowing patch might mean pests, thirsty roots or poor soil.

In India, drones are also used to spray crops. Under a government plan announced in 2023, called Namo Drone Didi, women in village self-help groups are trained as drone pilots and hired by farmers nearby. A drone can cover a hectare, a square 100 metres on each side, in about ten to fifteen minutes, and the person controlling it stays out of the spray. Drones are also being tried for spreading seed and fertiliser.

{{story:satellites-help}}

After Alexander died in 323 BCE, his generals split his empire. One of them, Seleucus, tried to win back land in the Indus region around 305 BCE. Instead, he made a [[treaty|treaty]] with Chandragupta.

Seleucus handed over large areas in the northwest. In return, Chandragupta sent him 500 [[@oddity-elephants-war|war elephants]], which Seleucus later used to win a major battle at home. The two sides also exchanged ambassadors. The Greek envoy Megasthenes lived at the Mauryan court and wrote a book called *Indica*. The book is lost, but other Greek and Roman writers quoted parts of it.

{{story:elephants-talk}}

In the middle of the Kremlin is Cathedral Square. Here stand three great cathedrals with golden domes. In the Cathedral of the Dormition, Russian tsars were crowned for centuries. In the Cathedral of the Archangel, many of them are buried.

Nearby is the Ivan the Great Bell Tower, about 81 metres tall, which was for a long time the tallest building in Moscow. At its foot sits the cracked Tsar Bell, about 200 tonnes, and the Tsar Cannon, a huge bronze cannon from 1586 that was never fired in battle.

{{story:cold-war}}
